  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe low-level stratus layer from the storm system yesterday has\ncontinued to stay primarily in southwestern Nebraska and \nwestern/south central Kansas. This cloud layer is expected to\ncontinue through the afternoon into the overnight hours, \npotentially expanding eastward as far as Highway 281. The clear \nskies that are dominating most of the local area, combined with \nsoutherly winds occasionally gusting to 20-25 MPH, will allow \ntemperatures to increase into the 50s this afternoon, and lows \nto fall down into the low to mid 30s overnight. There also is a \nslight chance (REFS probabilities of 20-40%) of some fog \ndevelopment overnight into tomorrow morning. However, there is \nlow confidence in this occurring, as winds speeds may limit this\nfog development.\n\nThe beginning of the work week will feature a dry, warming period, as\na ridge builds over the central US. High temperatures will climb\ninto the 50s tomorrow and into the 60s Wednesday afternoon. This\nwarm up trend will also be felt in the overnight low\ntemperatures, with lows generally in the 40s. These low\ntemperatures are well above normal for this time of year (normal\nlows around 27). Accompanying this warming trend will be\nincreasing southerly winds, as a surface high pressure center \nin the midwest intensifies and an approaching shortwave starts \nto cause pressure falls in the elevated terrain. Sustained\nsoutherly winds around 20-25 MPH will be accompanied with gusts\nup to 40-50 MPH, with NBM guidance even indicating a 10-20%\nchance of seeing wind gusts over 50 MPH.\n\nA small chance of rain returns to the forecast Wednesday night,\nas a shortwave trough crosses over the area. Along with these\nprecipitation chances, a cold front is expected to move across\nthe area, which may allow overnight lows to return to the 30s\nThursday morning. However, these more seasonal temperatures are\nnot expected to stick around, as highs are expected to increase\nback into the 60s Friday afternoon, along with wind gusts\nincreasing back up to 35-45 MPH.\n\nGreater precipitation chances return next weekend, as\nanother trough approaches the area supporting a low pressure\ncenter in the central plains. We could see some thunderstorms\ndevelop in the warm sector of this cyclone Friday evening, with\nsevere weather not out of the question. Most, if not all, of the\nprecipitation associated with this event is expected to fall as\nrain.",
